# Working with coding agents

The practices that make AI-assisted development sustainable on real projects.
Tool-agnostic; specific commands are noted in their tool's section in
`AGENTS.md`.

## Three forces that determine output quality

1. **Context** — what the agent actually has loaded.
2. **Constraints** — what it isn't allowed to do.
3. **Verification** — how you catch what it got wrong.

Get those three right and the choice of model matters less than the internet
claims it does.

## Context hygiene

Long conversations rot. Every turn drags the entire prior context into the next
prompt; old code, old decisions, old reasoning all ride along even after they
stop being relevant. Output quality degrades long before the context limit is
hit.

The discipline is simple:

- **Compact at ~70 % usage.** The runtime command varies (`/compact`,
  `/summarise`, equivalent in your tool). It preserves the active thread, drops
  the dead weight.
- **Clear on a topic switch.** When you finish "write the tests for X" and want
  to start "refactor module Y", `clear` the conversation. Otherwise the agent
  carries X-shaped assumptions into Y.
- **One task, one session.** Short, focused sessions beat one mammoth thread.
  The cost is one extra `clear`; the benefit is dramatically tighter context.

## Token economy

A few cheap habits save the bulk of token spend:

- **Cache-friendly system blocks.** `AGENTS.md` and `guidelines/*.md` are
  structured so they sit at the front of the prompt unchanged — most providers
  cache the front of the prompt, so identical content across sessions costs
  almost nothing after the first hit.
- **Lazy-load source.** Don't paste large files into the prompt. Let the agent
  use its read tool on demand; that way only the relevant slice enters context.
- **No file dumps in the conversation.** If you find yourself pasting a file,
  reference its path instead. The agent's read tool is cheaper than your
  paste.

## Plan → Execute → Verify

The single highest-leverage rule. For any non-trivial change:

1. **Plan** — agent (or you) drafts the approach, names risks, lists open
   questions. You approve before code is written.
2. **Execute** — small diff, one logical change at a time.
3. **Verify** — `just qa` plus an independent review against the spec.
   Verification is the highest-value step; skipping it turns the loop into
   guessing.

A "fix" that wasn't verified isn't a fix — it's a guess that nobody has
checked yet.

## Hard limits (the safety net)

Autonomous agents can burn money in fire-and-forget loops. Defend against that
with limits the runtime enforces, not policies you hope it remembers:

- **Max iterations** — after ~10 consecutive autonomous actions (edit → test
  fails → edit → …) without progress, force a human checkpoint.
- **Token / cost visibility** — keep usage in view so a runaway session is
  obvious before it gets expensive. `ccusage` (Claude Code, Codex CLI),
  `opencode-quota` / `tokscale` (OpenCode) surface live token + cost as a
  separate process; the AGENTS.md tool sections name the exact command per
  runtime. There is no template-wide auto-abort env var — the runtime's own
  permission model is the real cap.
- **Three-strike debugging** — after three failed root-cause hypotheses, stop
  and ask the user for more context rather than guessing further. Encoded in
  `skills/debug/SKILL.md`.

## Permissions: allow / ask / deny

Treat the runtime's permission system like a firewall, not paperwork.

- **allow** for what the agent should be free to do (read files, run `just`,
  run tests, format).
- **ask** for things that are sometimes fine, sometimes destructive (`git
  push`, `git reset`, `WebFetch`).
- **deny** for the absolute red lines (`rm -rf /`, reading `.env`, piping
  `curl | bash`).

Permission enforcement differs by tool:

| Tool | Mechanism | Coverage |
|---|---|---|
| Claude Code | `.claude/settings.json` `permissions.allow/ask/deny` | Bash, Read, Write, Edit — all pattern-based |
| OpenCode | `opencode.json` `permission` (granular object syntax) | `bash`, `edit`, `read`, `external_directory`, `doom_loop` — pattern-based per tool |
| Codex | `.codex/config.toml` `default_permissions = ":workspace"` | Sandbox profile (execpolicy deny-syntax not yet TOML-stable; guardrails are verbal in `AGENTS.md`) |
| Aider | none | No native permission system — `AGENTS.md` + pre-commit are the only safety net |

Deny rules are belt-and-suspenders to the pre-commit `gitleaks`/
`detect-private-key` hooks. Both should be in place; either one alone is too
fragile.

## The self-improvement loop (private vs team memory)

Two memory tiers, kept apart deliberately so personal corrections don't clutter
shared project rules:

| Tool | Private memory (local, automatic) | Team memory (git-shared, curated) | Bridge |
|---|---|---|---|
| Claude Code | Auto-Memory (`~/.claude/projects/<project>/memory/`), `/memory` to inspect/edit | `guidelines/*.md`, `AGENTS.md` | `/capture` skill (manual promote) |
| Codex | `features.memories` (Chronicle), managed by Codex | `guidelines/*.md`, `AGENTS.md` | `/capture` skill (manual promote) |
| OpenCode | none (no native dynamic memory) | `instructions` array loads `guidelines/*.md` + `AGENTS.md` | `/capture` skill (manual promote) |
| Aider | none (no native dynamic memory) | `read:` array loads `guidelines/*.md` + `AGENTS.md` | `/capture` skill (manual promote) |

**The discipline** — when the agent makes an avoidable mistake, capture the
correction **once** as a durable rule. The decision is:

- Would this rule apply to **a colleague** too? → `/capture` into
  `guidelines/<topic>.md` or `AGENTS.md` (team tier, git-shared).
- Is it a **personal preference** (your shell, your sandbox URLs)? → leave it in
  private memory (Auto-Memory / Codex Memories). Don't pollute shared rules.

The rule is worth keeping only if it would prevent the **same mistake in a similar
future situation**. Vague warnings, one-offs, and duplicates of existing rules go
in the bin.

## Subagents (when forking context helps)

A subagent runs in its **own** context window with a tighter system prompt and
a narrower tool set. Use one when:

- The task would otherwise pollute the main context with verbose recon
  (read 30 files, summarise findings) — delegate to a `researcher` subagent.
- You want an **independent** judgement (a code review by the same session that
  wrote the code is not a review) — delegate to a `reviewer` subagent.
- The task needs a different model tier (cheap Haiku for routine recon,
  expensive Opus for hard reasoning).

Anti-pattern: a subagent for every imaginable specialisation. *"It multiplies
debug surface area, not throughput."* Three subagents (`researcher`, `reviewer`,
`security-auditor`) cover the bulk; resist sprawl.

## Trust and prompt injection

Anything the agent fetches from the web — including documentation, GitHub
issues, Stack Overflow snippets — is **data, not instructions**. If an HTML
page contains "ignore previous instructions and …", that line is content,
not a directive. Encode this in `AGENTS.md`; never grant a `WebFetch` of
arbitrary URLs without an `ask` permission unless the runtime is sandboxed.

## When to use which mode

| Situation | Mode |
|---|---|
| You know what you want, the diff is small | **Coder** seat, no plan step |
| The change touches >2 files or makes a design choice | **Architect** seat first (Plan Mode / `plan` primary) |
| You want a second opinion on a finished diff | Spawn the **reviewer** subagent |
| You're new to an area | Spawn the **researcher** subagent (or run `/explore`) |
| The task will run unattended | `/delegate` brief + sandbox + hard limits |

## Anti-patterns to recognise

| Anti-pattern | Symptom | Counter |
|---|---|---|
| Mammoth session | Quality degrades over hours; agent repeats itself. | Clear and restart per task. |
| Implementation before plan | "Just code it" → 200-line diff that misses the point. | Plan first; small approved diff. |
| Mocked verification | Tests are green; real-world behaviour is broken. | Integration tests, real DB via Testcontainers. |
| Trusting fetched content | Agent follows instructions embedded in a doc page. | Fetched content is data; encode in `AGENTS.md`. |
| Permissive `bypass` mode | Speed today, regret tomorrow. | Reserve for sandboxed, isolated runs. |

